<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    The problem with keeping your whole life in one place is that your whole
    life is then in one place. Two hundred things, all of them yours, none
    of them urgent enough to have shouted.
  </p>
  <p>
    The dashboard is the answer to that: a short read of the week across
    everything you've switched on, saying what actually needs you and why it
    thinks so.
  </p>
</section>

<%= about_screenshot("dashboard") %>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What it puts in front of you</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Things with a date attached") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        What's coming this week and what has quietly gone past — appointments,
        renewals, deadlines from letters you filed weeks ago and stopped
        thinking about.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Things that have drifted") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The check that's overdue, the friend it's been eleven months since,
        the task you've moved to tomorrow nine times. Nothing here has a
        deadline; that's exactly why it needs surfacing.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Capture, right there") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The most common thing you'll want after reading it is to add
        something, so adding a task, an event, a note or a document is on
        the page rather than three clicks away.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Only what you turned on") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Switch a part of life off and it stops appearing here. The dashboard
        never nags you about an area you've told it you don't want.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Why one page and not six</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Because the collisions are the point. The week the car insurance renews
    is also the week of the school trip and the hospital appointment, and no
    amount of excellent per-category software will ever tell you that. It
    only shows up when one thing is looking at all of it.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">With and without a model</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Most of this is arithmetic on dates and needs no intelligence at all —
    it works on an installation with nothing configured. What a model adds
    is the summarising: reading across the week and saying the three things
    that matter in a sentence each, rather than handing you a longer list.
  </p>
</section>
